WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:02.850
Film Courage: So switching gears a little
bit, I was wondering if you can take us back

00:00:02.850 --> 00:00:07.879
to where you were in your life when you switched
careers? You were a basketball player for

00:00:07.879 --> 00:00:12.120
awhile. I know you grew up in the music business
with your dad but then you played basketball.

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:16.840
And then there was a point where you switched
careers? Can you talk about mentally where

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:21.210
you were, physically where you were? How was
that? Harvey Mason, Jr.: Yeah, when I was

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:26.199
playing basketball my dream was to play in
the NBA and I had a career-ending injury which

00:00:26.199 --> 00:00:32.600
kind of made me change direction and focus
or re-focus on music. And that was a tough

00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:38.440
time because I really planned on playing basketball
and I worked my whole life to do that. But

00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:43.010
in the long run I think it was a really fortunate
turn of events. To get hurt, you never think

00:00:43.010 --> 00:00:49.150
it’s a good thing, but it turned me toward
music and it made me really, really take all

00:00:49.150 --> 00:00:54.320
my energy and effort that I was putting into
basketball and put it toward music. That was

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:59.380
right at the time I finished college. So I
was young and I was still malleable enough

00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:05.460
to adjust and switch into something else.
But music was always a passion of mine when

00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:09.119
I was a kid. I think I started taking piano
lessons when I was 6 years old or 7 years

00:01:09.119 --> 00:01:16.509
old and it was a hobby all through college.
I always kept my toe in the water with music.

00:01:16.509 --> 00:01:20.090
And then when basketball finished for me,
it was just definitely kind of a wholesale

00:01:20.090 --> 00:01:25.361
refocusing. I started doing jingles locally
in Arizona where I was going to college. I

00:01:25.361 --> 00:01:30.950
started doing commercial music and working
for ad agencies. And I was doing music for

00:01:30.950 --> 00:01:36.340
anybody who wanted it. Brake stores, buses,
things like that. I would make a jingle for

00:01:36.340 --> 00:01:43.569
anybody. And all the while I was trying to
get to Los Angeles to kind of kick in somebody’s

00:01:43.569 --> 00:01:50.780
door to listen to my music. That took me probably
three or four years driving out to LA for

00:01:50.780 --> 00:01:55.500
a week at a time, trying to get people to
listen to my demos. I was sending cassettes

00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:59.759
to people back then, throwing cassettes into
people’s convertibles, doing anything I

00:01:59.759 --> 00:02:07.180
could to get my music heard and that was kind
of how I started here in LA was just driving

00:02:07.180 --> 00:02:12.270
out here. Sleeping in my car every now and
then, whatever I had to do. And then one guy

00:02:12.270 --> 00:02:17.540
took notice. He said “Hey I love what you
do. I’ll pay you (I think) $2,500.00 to

00:02:17.540 --> 00:02:21.180
do a job for me.” and at that point I was
like “Oh my God! I’m in. I made it.”

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:26.130
And that was my first job. Motown Records.
I did a remix for one of their artists. And

00:02:26.130 --> 00:02:30.599
then I ended up here doing an interview with
you. Film Courage: What year was this? Harvey

00:02:30.599 --> 00:02:38.189
Mason, Jr.: That was probably 1990….1994?
Something like that. Film Courage: You said

00:02:38.189 --> 00:02:43.799
earlier that you take a nugget of something
from each project and that there is [always]

00:02:43.799 --> 00:02:48.519
some lesson in it. So looking back at the
jingles…maybe creatively it wasn’t the

00:02:48.519 --> 00:02:52.490
most fulfilling, maybe it was? What did you
take from this [time working on jingles] that

00:02:52.490 --> 00:02:56.450
you then used to build this amazing studio
that you have here which is absolutely beautiful.

00:02:56.450 --> 00:03:01.180
Harvey Mason, Jr.: I’ll tell you, I took
so much from that two or three year period

00:03:01.180 --> 00:03:06.980
where I did advertising music because you
have to do every range of music. If some guy

00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:12.659
says “Well I want it to be country western
and my burger joint, I feel like it’s a

00:03:12.659 --> 00:03:17.379
country place or…blues music or I want this
kind of a music or I want classical.” You

00:03:17.379 --> 00:03:22.780
have to be able to do all that type of music.
You have to be able to deliver it quickly

00:03:22.780 --> 00:03:26.430
and you have to be able to do it economically
when you’re working in a small market like

00:03:26.430 --> 00:03:31.930
that so it was an amazing training ground
for me. The other thing that I got from that

00:03:31.930 --> 00:03:38.200
was working outside of your own bubble of
creativity. Like you can’t do whatever you

00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:41.981
want. When this guy is trying to sell cars
with his music. You have to do whatever he

00:03:41.981 --> 00:03:48.310
thinks is going to sell cars. So you really
have to pay attention to what people want.

00:03:48.310 --> 00:03:53.930
You have to be able to translate what they’re
thinking because a car salesman or a break

00:03:53.930 --> 00:03:59.370
store owner or a guy who runs a mall is not
a musician. You have to figure out what he’s

00:03:59.370 --> 00:04:03.370
trying to get to with his music and figure
out how to get it out of your fingers and

00:04:03.370 --> 00:04:07.970
out of the speakers ultimately. That is what
I use everyday whether it is an A &amp; R guy

00:04:07.970 --> 00:04:14.620
for a record label or a director for a film.
Being able to take what they say and they

00:04:14.620 --> 00:04:19.910
think and what they feel and making that come
to life with music is something that I do

00:04:19.910 --> 00:04:23.780
every day. So that is great training. And
as I said, being able to do all the different

00:04:23.780 --> 00:04:31.440
kind of genres of music and being able to
have access to my background being in all

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:37.090
those types of music. I did so many rock n’
roll commercials, so many classical commercials

00:04:37.090 --> 00:04:40.530
that I can draw from when I’m needing to
do a piece of music for a film. Question for

00:04:40.530 --> 00:04:40.719
the Viewers: What was your first job in the
entertainment business?

